From 70ce03ece47660e8d20c7e8159ae4f2da0136aa0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Fountain5405 <Fountain5405@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 13:23:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] dalek-ff-group: Field25519 as four saturated 64-bit limbs

Field25519 (2^255 - 19; Helios coordinates and Selene scalars in FCMP++)
used crypto-bigint's generic Montgomery arithmetic. On lowmem-v2 at
128-input blocks, montgomery_reduction<4>, mul_wide<4> and the generic pow
were about 49% of CPU on a Core 2 Quad Q6600 node on an SSD.

The element is now four saturated 64-bit limbs. Any value below 2^256 is a
valid representation, and products are reduced with 2^256 = 38 (mod p). The
size (32 bytes) and alignment are unchanged, which the FFI requires:
fcmp_pp_rust, fcmp++.h and fcmp_pp_types.h assert a 32-byte, pointer-aligned
SeleneScalar.
- Equality: the difference is 0, p or 2p (results are below 2^256 < 3p).
- to_repr, is_odd and the other observable outputs go through one
  canonicalization (fold bit 255, then one conditional subtraction of p).
  from_repr accepts exactly the values below p, as 0.5.0 does.
- invert, sqrt and the (p-5)/8 power use curve25519-dalek's pow22501
  addition chain instead of the generic 4-bit-window pow.
- #[inline] on the crate's shared operator macro (lib.rs).
This replaces an earlier radix-2^51 attempt in the same file, which is not
part of this series: its 40-byte elements broke the FFI's 32-byte layout.

Bounds (reviewed line by line by a second reviewer, who
found them sound):
- adc/mac: the peak mac value is 2^128 - 1, which fits u128;
- folding the high half: for k <= 38 the low limb wraps to below 38k, so no
  second wrap;
- sub: the second-borrow fix is correct mod p, and its final d0 - 38 cannot
  underflow;
- squaring: the pre-shifted top limb loses no bits;
- canonicalization: the q test (value + 19, carry out) is exact, and one
  conditional subtraction suffices after the bit-255 fold.

Constant time: no data-dependent branches or secret-indexed loads.
Disassembled on x86-64 at opt-level 3, codegen-units 1, overflow-checks off:
add, sub, mul, square, to_repr, ct_eq and wide_reduce are branch-free;
invert and sqrt run fixed trip counts; pow's branches test only public loop
state, and its table select uses ct_eq and conditional_select. This was
inspection, not proof, and holds per compilation. fcmp_pp_rust's release
profile builds with overflow-checks on, and that build has not been
re-inspected. No statistical timing test has been done.

Tests:
- New: constants against their definitions; limb edge values (p, p+1, 2p,
  2^256-1, double-borrow subtractions); the addition chains against the
  generic pow.
- The crate's tests, including ff-group-tests' prime_field suite, pass in
  release and in debug with overflow checks.
- Differential against unmodified 0.5.0, compared by canonical encoding
  after every step, overflow checks on: 5,000,000 random operations over a
  pool seeded with edge values (add, sub, mul, square, neg, double, invert,
  sqrt, sqrt_ratio_i, pow, select, eq, is_odd, is_zero, from_u128,
  sum/product), 200,000 encodings and 200,000 wide reductions: 0 mismatches.
  The second reviewer's own boundary harness also found 0 mismatches.
- The FCMP++ crate suite passes on top of it, including malleated and
  tampered proofs.

Speed:
- Field operations, one core of a 6-core Ryzen 5 5600G (14 GB RAM), best of 5: mul 1.98x,
  square 2.16x, add 1.12x, sub 1.13x, invert 2.63x; eq 0.67x (it now needs a
  reduction).
- FCMP++ verification per proof at crate level (1, 2, 8 and 16 inputs):
  -22.4 to -24.2%.
- Node, stock beta.3.1 with only this crate changed (5600G): wall -15.3% and
  CPU -19.4% on small-transaction blocks; wall -6.5% and CPU -7.3% on
  128-input blocks; memory unchanged.
- With lowmem-v2 (static, 5600G): CPU -27.9% on 128-input blocks and -18.8%
  on small-transaction blocks.
- The Core 2 Quad node: wall -25% on 128-input blocks against lowmem-v2.

